.\" Man page for aspen — a fast, byte-compatible reimplementation of tree(1).
.\" This page is original to the aspen project (it is NOT derived from the
.\" GPL tree(1) manual). Written for aspen 0.1.0.
.TH ASPEN 1 "2026-06-08" "aspen 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
aspen, asp \- list the contents of directories in a tree-like format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aspen
[\fIoptions\fR]
[\fIdirectory\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aspen
recursively lists the contents of each given
.I directory
(the current directory if none is given) as an indented tree, then prints a
summary count of the directories and files shown.
.PP
It is a from-scratch C reimplementation of
.BR tree (1)
that is designed to be a drop-in replacement: for every supported invocation its
standard output, standard error, and exit status are
.B byte-for-byte identical
to GNU tree 2.3.2, while running measurably faster. The only intentional
differences are the program name itself (\fBaspen\fR appears in usage, version,
and error text) and a small set of cases where tree's output is deterministic but
self-inconsistent or wrong, which aspen renders correctly instead; these are
enumerated under
.BR COMPATIBILITY .
.PP
The speed comes from avoiding work tree always does \(em most importantly,
.B aspen
does not
.BR stat (2)
an entry when the directory read already reports its type, so a plain listing
issues far fewer system calls.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Listing options
.TP
.B \-a
List all files, including those whose names begin with a dot.
.TP
.B \-d
List directories only.
.TP
.B \-l
Follow symbolic links that point to directories, as if they were directories.
A link that would create a cycle is reported but not followed.
.TP
.B \-f
Print the full path prefix for each file.
.TP
.B \-x
Stay on the current filesystem only, as with
.BR "find \-xdev" .
.TP
.BI \-L " level"
Descend at most
.I level
directories deep.
.TP
.B \-R
At the maximum
.B \-L
depth, rerun
.B aspen
in each leaf directory and write the result to a
.B 00Tree.html
file there. Only meaningful with the HTML/text output.
.TP
.BI \-P " pattern"
List only files matching the wildcard
.IR pattern .
The pattern is matched against the basename, the full path, and each
\fB/\fR-delimited suffix of the path;
.B *
does not cross
.BR / ,
but
.B **
does.
.TP
.BI \-I " pattern"
Do not list files matching the wildcard
.IR pattern .
.TP
.B \-\-gitignore
Filter entries using the
.B .gitignore
files found along the walk (and
.BR $GIT_DIR/info/exclude ).
.TP
.BI \-\-gitfile " file"
Read an additional gitignore-format
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-case
Match
.B \-P
and
.B \-I
patterns case-insensitively.
.TP
.B \-\-matchdirs
Apply
.B \-P
patterns to directory names as well, showing a matched directory's whole subtree.
.TP
.B \-\-metafirst
Print the metadata columns at the start of each line rather than before the name.
.TP
.B \-\-prune
Omit empty directories from the output (implied by some filters).
.TP
.B \-\-info
Print descriptive text from
.B .info
files found alongside the listed entries.
.TP
.BI \-\-infofile " file"
Read an additional
.B .info
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-\-noreport
Suppress the directory/file count printed after the tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-charset " X"
Use character set
.I X
for the indentation glyphs and for HTML output.
.TP
.BI \-\-filelimit " #"
Do not descend into directories containing more than
.I #
entries.
.TP
.B \-\-condense
Collapse chains of single-child directories onto one line.
.TP
.BI \-o " filename"
Write output to
.I filename
instead of standard output.
.SS File options
.TP
.B \-q
Print non-printable characters in names as
.BR ? .
.TP
.B \-N
Print non-printable characters in names verbatim.
.TP
.B \-Q
Quote each name with double quotes.
.TP
.B \-p
Print the file type and permission bits for each entry.
.TP
.B \-u
Print the owning user name (or UID).
.TP
.B \-g
Print the owning group name (or GID).
.TP
.B \-s
Print the size of each file in bytes.
.TP
.B \-h
Print sizes in a human-readable form (powers of 1024).
.TP
.B \-\-si
Like
.BR \-h ,
but in SI units (powers of 1000).
.TP
.B \-\-du
Report each directory's size as the cumulative size of its contents.
.TP
.B \-D
Print the last-modification time, or with
.B \-c
the last status-change time.
.TP
.BI \-\-timefmt " format"
Print times formatted by
.IR format ,
as in
.BR strftime (3).
.TP
.B \-F
Append a type indicator (one of
.BR "/ = * @ | >" )
to each name, as in
.BR "ls \-F" .
.TP
.B \-\-inodes
Print the inode number of each file.
.TP
.B \-\-device
Print the device number each file resides on.
.SS Sorting options
.TP
.B \-v
Sort names by embedded version numbers (natural order).
.TP
.B \-t
Sort by last-modification time.
.TP
.B \-c
Sort by last status-change time.
.TP
.B \-U
Do not sort; list entries in directory order.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse the sort order.
.TP
.B \-\-dirsfirst
List directories before files (disabled by
.BR \-U ).
.TP
.B \-\-filesfirst
List files before directories (disabled by
.BR \-U ).
.TP
.BI \-\-sort " X"
Select the sort key:
.BR name ", " version ", " size ", " mtime ", " ctime ", or " none .
.SS Graphics options
.TP
.B \-i
Omit the indentation lines.
.TP
.B \-A
Use ANSI line-drawing characters for indentation.
.TP
.B \-S
Use CP437 (DOS console) line-drawing characters.
.TP
.B \-n
Never colorize the output (overridden by
.BR \-C ).
.TP
.B \-C
Always colorize the output, honouring
.B LS_COLORS
/
.BR TREE_COLORS .
.TP
.BI \-\-compress " #"
Compress the indentation by
.I #
columns.
.SS XML / HTML / JSON / hyperlink options
.TP
.B \-X
Emit an XML representation of the tree.
.TP
.B \-J
Emit a JSON representation of the tree.
.TP
.BI \-H " baseHREF"
Emit HTML, using
.I baseHREF
as the base directory for links.
.TP
.BI \-T " string"
Replace the default HTML title and H1 heading with
.IR string .
.TP
.B \-\-nolinks
Do not emit hyperlinks in HTML output.
.TP
.BI \-\-hintro " file"
Use
.I file
as the HTML preamble.
.TP
.BI \-\-houtro " file"
Use
.I file
as the HTML epilogue.
.TP
.B \-\-hyperlink
Emit OSC 8 terminal hyperlinks around each name.
.TP
.BI \-\-scheme " X"
Set the OSC 8 hyperlink URI scheme (default
.BR file:// ).
.TP
.BI \-\-authority " X"
Set the OSC 8 hyperlink authority (host).
.SS Input options
.TP
.B \-\-fromfile
Read the tree structure from a list of paths rather than the filesystem
.RB ( .
reads standard input).
.TP
.B \-\-fromtabfile
Read the tree from a tab-indented file
.RB ( .
reads standard input).
.TP
.B \-\-fflinks
Honour link information when reading with
.BR \-\-fromfile .
.SS Miscellaneous options
.TP
.B \-\-opt\-toggle
Enable option toggling.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
Stop option processing; treat the rest of the command line as directory
arguments.
.SH "ASPEN EXTENSIONS"
These options and environment variables are specific to
.B aspen
and have no counterpart in tree. They never change the output bytes; they only
affect how the work is scheduled, so a run with any of them remains byte-identical
to the default.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " N"
Bound the metadata-stat worker pool to
.I N
threads
.RB ( 0 ,
the default, auto-detects). The pool is used only when a flag forces a
.BR stat (2)
of every entry and a directory is large enough to amortize it; output ordering is
deterministic regardless of
.IR N .
This option is intentionally omitted from
.B \-\-help
so that the usage text stays byte-identical to tree.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A usage or option error.
.TP
.B 2
One or more directories could not be opened, or a
.B \-\-filelimit
was exceeded. Unlike tree, aspen reports this consistently in every output mode.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LC_ALL ", " LC_COLLATE ", " LANG
Select the locale used for the default name sort. In the
.B C
locale aspen sorts bytewise; otherwise it collates per the locale.
.TP
.B LS_COLORS ", " TREE_COLORS ", " CLICOLOR ", " CLICOLOR_FORCE ", " NO_COLOR
Control colorization under
.BR \-C "/" \-n .
.TP
.B GIT_DIR
With
.BR \-\-gitignore ,
the location of
.B info/exclude
to consult.
.TP
.B ASP_IO
Select the metadata-stat backend:
.B threads
(the default thread pool),
.B serial
(force the inline path), or
.B uring
(Linux io_uring, when available). All produce identical output.
.TP
.B ASP_PREFETCH
If set, enable an experimental cross-directory read-prefetch pool. Off by default;
it can help on high-latency storage but is slightly slower on a warm cache.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B aspen
targets byte-for-byte parity with GNU tree 2.3.2 on standard output, standard
error, and exit status. The deliberate exceptions are:
.IP \(bu 2
the program name
.RB ( aspen )
in usage, version, and diagnostic text;
.IP \(bu 2
a small set of cases where tree's output is deterministic yet wrong or
self-inconsistent \(em for example, a non-zero exit status is reported in every
mode when a directory cannot be opened, JSON stays well-formed after an error, and
percent-encoded hyperlinks use the real byte rather than a sign-extended one.
.PP
Behaviour that tree drives by undefined or order-dependent means (such as dropping
an entry whose
.BR lstat (2)
races a concurrent unlink) is not reproduced; aspen produces a deterministic
result instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B aspen
List the current directory as a tree.
.TP
.B aspen \-ah /etc
List
.I /etc
including dot files, with human-readable sizes.
.TP
.B aspen \-P '*.c' \-\-prune src
Show only C source files, pruning directories that contain none.
.TP
.B aspen \-J \-o tree.json .
Write a JSON representation of the current directory to
.IR tree.json .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tree (1),
.BR ls (1),
.BR find (1)
.SH AUTHORS
.B aspen
is an independent reimplementation of the tree utility. It is not affiliated with
or derived from the source of GNU tree; it aims only for compatibility with tree's
observable behaviour.
